  "summary": "The first Slavic alphabet — round mystical letters carved on Croatian churches for eight centuries.",
  "description": "Glagolitic, devised for the mission of Cyril and Methodius, is Europe's oldest Slavic script and one of its most beautiful: round, knot-like letterforms built from circle, cross and triangle that medieval writers said encoded the Trinity. While most Slavic lands switched to Cyrillic, Croatian coastal towns and islands (Baška, Hum, Krk) kept carving and illuminating Glagolitic for eight centuries — the 1,100-line Baška tablet of c. 1100 is a national monument — leaving a landscape dotted with stone inscriptions, liturgical books, and even the 16th-century \"Alphabet Town\" of Roč. It reads today as both writing and sacred ornament.",
  "culturalContext": "Glagolitic made Croatian coastal identity: papal permission for the Old Church Slavonic rite (1248) let islands and Istria keep the script for eight centuries, the Baška tablet became national monument, and the Roč-Hum \"Alley of Glagolitic letters\" turned letterforms into landscape — writing as both liturgy and Croatian distinctiveness within the Slavic world.",
